./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md-216-- `def-real-exponential-function-and-e` must carry `justified_by: [lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]` when authored, because convergence is discharged by the following lemma.
./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md-217-- The construction order is binding: series convergence → Cauchy-product addition → reciprocal/positivity → derivative → monotonicity/range → IVP and functional characterizations. Reversing these arrows risks circular proofs.
./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md-218-- `thm-exponential-addition-formula` must perform the embedded-natural coefficient calculation. It cannot simply identify the Cauchy product with the exponential series in prose.
./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md:219:- `thm-exponential-product-limit` uses a fixed finite head and an exponential-series tail. Coefficientwise convergence alone is insufficient to interchange the growing finite sum and limit.
./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md-220-- `thm-normalized-exponential-functional-equation` states its normalization in both clauses. Without (F'(0)=1), or alternatively (F(1)=e) plus continuity, the multiplicative Cauchy equation has a one-parameter family of regular solutions.
./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md-221-- `thm-picard-iterates-for-exponential` proves each iterate is the exact partial sum before taking a compact-uniform limit. The uniform-limit integration theorem, not pointwise convergence, passes the limit through the integral.
./research/frontier-6-batch-2.notes.md-222-- The flat-function derivative estimate must reduce odd powers of (1/x) to an integer-power bound; it may not use undeveloped arbitrary real powers.
